.TH M-FLASHER 1 "" "M-Flasher" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
m-flasher \- back up, restore and reinstall the firmware of M-VAVE FM-1 and SMK-37 synthesizers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B m-flasher
.br
.B m-flasher
.RI [ options ]
.B find
|
.B download\-mode
|
.B restart
|
.B info
|
.B backup
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B m-flasher
.RI [ options ]
.B restore
.I backup.bin
.br
.B m-flasher
.RI [ options ]
.B firmware
.I package.fwsc
.RB [ \-\-with\-bootloader ]
.RB [ \-\-wipe\-settings ]
.br
.B m-flasher
.RI [ options ]
.B bootloader
.RB [ \-\-stock
.IR package.fwsc " | "
.B \-\-file
.IR uboot.boot ]
.br
.B m-flasher
.RI [ options ]
.B run\-ram
.I image
.BI \-\-addr " addr"
.RB [ \-\-call
.IR opcode ]...
.RB [ \-\-read
.IR addr:len
.B \-o
.IR file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
M-Flasher backs up, restores and reinstalls the firmware of an M-VAVE FM-1 or
SMK-37 Pro / Elite over USB, and installs m-bootloader. It talks to the
chip's built-in USB download mode, so it also works when the firmware on the
unit is broken. Without arguments it opens its window; with arguments it is a
command-line tool.
.PP
Put the unit in download mode and connect it with a USB data cable: with
M-VAVE's firmware or Felucca running, run
.BR "m-flasher download-mode" ;
with m-bootloader, hold REC (SMK-37: SEQ REC) while switching it on; with
Felucca, SLOOP or Snoop37 running, hold OCT\- and OCT+ for 5 seconds.
.PP
Changes are shown first and need a typed
.BR yes .
A full backup is saved before the first write, and everything written is
verified.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B find
List the units in download mode, and those running, on USB MIDI.
.TP
.B download\-mode
Switch the unit that is running, connected over USB MIDI, to download mode:
send it the SysEx F0 22 24 35 7D F7 and wait for it to come back.
.TP
.B restart
Leave download mode: upload a short program to the chip's RAM that resets
the unit, which then starts its firmware; the flash is not touched. Waits up
to 60 s for the firmware on USB MIDI.
.TP
.B info
Read the unit's flash and show the model, firmware and bootloader.
.TP
.BR backup " [\fIdir\fR]"
Save the whole flash and a .json describing it.
.TP
.BI restore " backup.bin"
Write the parts of a backup that differ from the unit.
.TP
.BI firmware " package.fwsc"
Install the firmware from an M-VAVE package;
.B \-\-with\-bootloader
also writes its stock bootloader,
.B \-\-wipe\-settings
erases the settings and Bluetooth pairings.
.TP
.B bootloader
Install m-bootloader for the identified model, or the stock one from a
package
.RB ( \-\-stock ),
or a bootloader file
.RB ( \-\-file ).
.TP
.BI run\-ram " image " \-\-addr " addr"
Upload a program of your own to the chip's RAM (addr within
0x01C20000..0x01C2FFFF, the image ending before 0x01C30000), start it, and
talk to it; it does not touch the flash and does not start M-Flasher's flash
loader. It runs arbitrary code on the chip, and switching the unit off and on
returns it to normal.
.B \-\-call
.I opcode
(16-bit hex, repeatable) sends the command and prints the 16-byte answer;
.B \-\-read
.I addr:len
.B \-o
.I file
reads RAM from the program with FD61 commands of at most 4096 bytes. See
.B m-flasher run\-ram \-\-help
and the documentation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-device " path"
The unit to use, when more than one is connected.
.TP
.BR \-\-board " fm1|smk37"
The model, when it cannot be identified for certain.
.TP
.BI \-\-backup\-dir " dir"
Where backups go (default: ~/Documents/M-Flasher/backups).
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Do not ask before writing.
.TP
.B \-\-restart
With
.BR restore ", " firmware " and " bootloader :
leave download mode once written, as
.BR restart .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-m-flasher.rules
Gives the user logged in at the computer access to a unit in download mode.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/jvitkauskas/m-flasher
